<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Münster Rebellion</h1><p class="st-meta">1534 CE – 1535 CE · capital Münster</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The radical Anabaptist regime that seized Münster in 1534 and proclaimed a 'New Jerusalem' before being crushed in 1535.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Münster, capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-munster">Prince-Bishopric of Münster</a>, had turned Lutheran under the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-rothmann">Bernhard Rothmann</a>. Early in 1534 radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a>, many from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, won control of the council. Led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> baker <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-matthys">Jan Matthys</a>, they expelled those who refused rebaptism and declared Münster the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-waldeck">Bishop Franz von Waldeck</a> laid siege to the city. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-matthys">Matthys</a> was killed in a sortie at Easter 1534, the tailor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden">Jan of Leiden</a> proclaimed himself king. The regime abolished private property, introduced polygamy and ruled through prophecy and terror, while hunger spread within the walls.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In June 1535 the besiegers, helped by deserters, stormed the city. The leaders were tortured and executed in January 1536, and their bodies were hung in iron cages from the tower of Saint Lambert's church, where the cages still hang. The episode discredited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptism</a> for generations.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/munster-rebellion.jpg" alt="Münster Rebellion" width="320" height="171"><figcaption>Image: Erhard Schön · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuensterUnderSiege1534.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1534 CE – 1535 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Münster</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jan Matthys, Jan of Leiden, Bernhard Knipperdolling</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anabaptism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German, Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anabaptist Kingdom of Münster, New Jerusalem</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1534&amp;focus=munster-rebellion" aria-label="Münster Rebellion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4852 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Münster Rebellion lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5172 638L5172 638"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5172 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5172 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5172 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Münster. <a href="/atlas?year=1534&amp;focus=munster-rebellion">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
